# vim:fileencoding=utf-8:ft=conf
#
# Configuration for the ssh kitten. See
# https://sw.kovidgoyal.net/kitty/kittens/ssh/
#
# The host list is rendered from the sshHosts of every machine in
# .chezmoidata/machines.toml, so a machine added there is covered here without
# another edit.

# On the machines this repository sets up, start the wrapper that a local kitty
# window starts, not the recorded login shell. The login shell would hand over
# to the pixi zsh from ~/.zprofile or ~/.bash_profile, and that exec throws away
# the shell integration the kitten had just installed. A session with no
# integration never reports its directory, so new_window_with_cwd (ctrl+a)
# passes the local directory to the remote, and the kitten's bootstrap fails on
# it. A login_shell whose name is zsh makes the kitten take its zsh branch
# directly, so the integration is installed in the shell that stays running.
#
# The wrapper, not the pixi zsh itself, because the kitten execs a named
# login_shell without checking that it exists: with a broken pixi zsh the
# session would end at once, where the wrapper falls back to the login shell.
# ~/.local/libexec/zsh and ~/.sh_utils/CAVEATS.md have the rest.
#
# Only these hosts, not `hostname *`. A machine this repository has not set up
# has no wrapper, and the kitten would fail on it in the same way; there the
# kitten's default, the login shell, is right. For the same reason, a machine
# of yours needs one `chezmoi update` over plain ssh before `kitten ssh` into it
# works again, once this file names the wrapper and the machine does not have it
# yet.
#
# The kitten writes these values into a script that the remote sh sources,
# inside double quotes, so $HOME is expanded on the remote and no account name
# is needed here. Checked against kittens/ssh/main.go and config.go in kitty
# 0.49.0 on 2026-09-21. Spellings that look simpler do not work: ~ is not
# expanded inside those quotes, a bare name is only looked up on sshd's PATH
# and in a fixed list of system directories, and $( is escaped, so no test can
# be written into the value either.
#
# hostname is matched against the name typed on the command line, which is the
# alias in ~/.ssh/config. Names are separated by spaces.
{{- $hosts := list }}
{{- range $name, $m := .machines }}
{{-   $hosts = concat $hosts $m.sshHosts }}
{{- end }}
{{- if $hosts }}
hostname {{ $hosts | join " " }}
login_shell $HOME/.local/libexec/zsh
{{- end }}
